The following is the complete history of the engine rebuilds that both my car and my 
friend
<A HREF="../owners/andy_giles.html">Andy Giles’</A> car have undergone.
<BR><BR>
The work was scheduled to take about 6 to 8 weeks and produce cars with about 220-230 bhp. However it actually took over
31 weeks (8 months) to complete. Andy Giles' car produces 196 bhp and 158 lbs/ft of
torque and cost &pound;3750. My car produces 181 bhp and 164 lbs/ft of
torque and cost
<A HREF="cost.html">&pound;5798</A>. The work on my car was more costly mainly
because:
<UL>
<LI>different con rods were used.
<LI>a custom exhaust had to be manufactured
<LI>a new fuel pump and fuel pressure regulator had to be fitted
<LI>I opted for the larger throttle body to give better low down torque
<LI>my price includes a Unichip piggy back ECU.
</UL>
The guys who carried out the work are called Mike and Simon and the company
is called MRH Engineering based in Strood, Kent.
<BR><BR>


<TABLE BORDER="0" CELLPADDING="4" CELLSPACING="4">

<TR><TD COLSPAN=2 BGCOLOR="#f00020"><CENTER>January 2001</CENTER></TD><TD>&nbsp</TD></TR>


<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Saturday&nbsp;6th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Received 2nd draft of Particulars of Claim document off Barrister. It still contains
some mistakes and he did not implement all of my changes. Wrote letter to Solicitor
asking them to make the necessary changes as I'm not prepared to wait again for
the Barrister to make them. Once they have done this I informed them to go send the
document off to the court as soon as possible.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Wednesday&nbsp;3rd</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Solicitor sent Barrister a letter copied to me asking them for the ammended draft
of the particulars of claim.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR><TD COLSPAN=2 BGCOLOR="#f00020"><CENTER>December 2000</CENTER></TD><TD>&nbsp</TD></TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Monday&nbsp;18th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Phoned Solicitor for 5 minutes and asked if they had received document off
barrister yet. They hadn't so asked them to chase them up as it's been 4 weeks.
The barrister was not waiting for anything from us and had not yet actually
requested copies of quotes.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Monday&nbsp;11th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Phoned Anvanti Motorsport and Palmer and Phillips and reminded them that I need
written copies of the quotes they gave me including estimated time for work.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Saturday&nbsp;9th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Got letter from Solicitor asking for copies of the quotes as the barrister may
want them.
</TD>
</TR>


<TR><TD COLSPAN=2 BGCOLOR="#f00020"><CENTER>November 2000</CENTER></TD><TD>&nbsp</TD></TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Thursday&nbsp;30th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Got receipt off barrister for &pound;250 + VAT.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Friday&nbsp;24th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Solicitor phoned to tell me off for sending letter directly to
barrister - not the correct eticate. Told solicitor to get barrister to
make necessary changes as per my letter.
</TD>
</TR>


<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Wednesday&nbsp;22nd</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Sent off letter to barrister detailing changes I want him to make to
the draft of particulars.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Tuesday&nbsp;21st</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Phoned up car rental companies for day rates.
Made final changes to letter to barrister detailing changes required to the draft
particulars of claim document.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Monday&nbsp;20th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Got third quote from Evolution Engineering.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Saturday&nbsp;18th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Got letter off solicitor confirming payment. They have paid barrister for writing
draft particulars of claim. Hourly rate increased to &pound125 + VAT an hour.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Thursday&nbsp;16th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Paid solicitor &pound;763.75 for work carried out between 15th June 2000 and 29th
Se[tember 2000. It included 250 + VAT for barrister to write particulars of claim.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Wednesday&nbsp;15th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Got quote off Palmer &amp; Phillips for &pound3438 and it would take them two days.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Monday&nbsp;6th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Send email off to Avanti Motorsport asking them to add the time it would take to
replace the engine to thier quote.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Friday&nbsp;3rd</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Chased up garages I sent requests for quotes to.
</TD>
</TR>


<TR><TD COLSPAN=2 BGCOLOR="#f00020"><CENTER>October 2000</CENTER></TD><TD>&nbsp</TD></TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Tuesday&nbsp;31st</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Received copy of barristers fees which were already included as
disbursements in previous letter from barrister.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Friday&nbsp;27th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Received invoice from solicitor for &pound;763.75. This included &pound;250 + VAT
disbursement charged by the barrister for the draft of particulars letter.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Wednesday&nbsp;18th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Got first quote back from Avanti Motorsport for &pound;2914 including VAT but no
details of how long it would take.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Tuesday&nbsp;3rd</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Posted letters to six garages asking them for a quote on the purchase of
a replacement engine, the fitting of it and how long it would take.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR><TD COLSPAN=2 BGCOLOR="#f00020"><CENTER>September 2000</CENTER></TD><TD>&nbsp</TD></TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Friday&nbsp;29th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Received letter off my solicitor confirming that the letter I propose to
send to the garages is okay.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Tuesday&nbsp;19th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Wrote letter I want to send to various garages asking them to quote for the
cost of fitting a replacement engine. Sent the letter via email to
my solicitor
to make sure it was neutral and that it asked for correct information.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Thursday&nbsp;14th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Finally received draft particulars of claim form from my barrister. Reads
reasonably well and quite weighty.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR><TD COLSPAN=2 BGCOLOR="#f00020"><CENTER>July 2000</CENTER></TD><TD>&nbsp</TD></TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Thursday&nbsp;13th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Went to see solicitor and stated that I wanted to go ahead with
legal proceedings. They said they would get a barrister to
draft a Particulars of Claim form together for me which I would check
before sending it to the Judge.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Monday&nbsp;10th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
MRH contacted my solictor requesting an update. My solicitor told them
the situation was the same as the letter sent on 29th June.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR><TD COLSPAN=2 BGCOLOR="#f00020"><CENTER>June 2000</CENTER></TD><TD>&nbsp</TD></TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Thursday&nbsp;29th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Had meeting with solicitor in morning.
My solictor sent a letter to MRH stating that I did not agree that the
work had been completed in time. It also said that I had sought on
numerous occasions in the past to arrange a date for a test session
but did not receive a satisfactory response. The letter went on to say that
therefore we are taking them to court and gave them seven days to
inform us if the address to issue proceedings against was wrong.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Monday&nbsp;26th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
My solictor received a fax from MRH. They explained that as far as they
were concerned their work had been completed within the time frame
and they wanted to know if I would attend a test session.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Friday&nbsp;16th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Received copy of the letter solictitor sent to MRH.
</TD>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Thursday&nbsp;15th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Went to see solicitor in moring.
My solictor sent a letter to MRH which explained that, as far as I was
concerned, they had failed to provide proof within the allocated time that
they could produce 200 bhp from the engine. The agreement signed in
January had provided for
this eventuality. My solicitor asked MRH for a response within seven
days for their proposals to comply with the agreement. The letter
also stated
that if we do not receive a satisfactory response then we would take
proceedings to county court.
</TD>
</TR>


<TR><TD COLSPAN=2 BGCOLOR="#f00020"><CENTER>May 2000</CENTER></TD><TD>&nbsp</TD></TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Tuesday&nbsp30th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Sent letter to MRH backing up previous email to say when the rolling road
will be available and that it would be the only chance they would have
to test the car.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Friday&nbsp26th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Spoke to Mark at Owen Developments and he said that MRH had not been
in contact with him with regard to a test session although they had
been in contact with Owen Developments with regard to Owen Devlepments
building a turbo for MRH. Mark said that next week was fully booked but
he would be able to fit the car in for testing anytime between 5th
and 9th June. Sent email to MRH saying that they should book the
car in between these times as it would be the only chance they would get.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Thursday&nbsp25th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Sent email to MRH asking if they had booked a test session at Owen
Developments rolling road.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Thursday&nbsp18th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Sent letter to MRH agreeing to attend the test session that they have planned
in order to resolve this issue as quickly as possible. Reminded them
that I had not received details of what changes they have made to the engine
and the specification of the engine they want to test.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Wednesday&nbsp10th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Asked MRH via email for details of the work they have carried out over the
12 week period and the specification of the engine they want to test.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Tuesday&nbsp9th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Received email from MRH explaining why they had not been able to develop the
engine on a rolling road. They plan on testing the engine and would like me
to attend.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR><TD COLSPAN=2 BGCOLOR="#f00020"><CENTER>April 2000</CENTER></TD><TD>&nbsp</TD></TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Thursday&nbsp27th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Sent letter to MRH saying I was not happy and that they had missed the deadline
for providing proof that they can build a 200 bhp engine. Asked them for a date when I
could bring my car down to have a standard engine fitted.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Wednesday&nbsp19th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Two days before the deadline MRH sent an email saying that the engine
was ready for testing but because of Easter they would like me to
contact them after the holiday.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Tuesday&nbsp18th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Sent letter to MRH reminding them that I had not received proof and they only
have until Friday 21 April.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Monday&nbsp10th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
10 Weeks into deadline and sent letter to MRH reminding them of the time scale
they are working to. Expressed concern that during this 10 week period I'd only
recived 2 updates.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR><TD COLSPAN=2 BGCOLOR="#f00020"><CENTER>March 2000</CENTER></TD><TD>&nbsp</TD></TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Friday&nbsp17th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Received email from MRH saying that the last week had seen zero progress on
the engine.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Tuesday&nbsp14th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Sent email saying MRH that they are 7 weeks into the 12 week period. Asked for
detailed status report by end of week containing work carried out so far
and plans for the remaining 5 weeks.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR><TD COLSPAN=2 BGCOLOR="#f00020"><CENTER>February 2000</CENTER></TD><TD>&nbsp</TD></TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Thursday&nbsp24th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
MRH phoned to say that they were going to try a different cam shaft but they
did not know how it compared either the standard camshaft or to the one
they fitted to the engine.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR><TD COLSPAN=2 BGCOLOR="#f00020"><CENTER>January 2000</CENTER></TD><TD>&nbsp</TD></TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Thursday&nbsp27th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Travelled to London to meet Andy and MRH. We moved Andy's car from it's workshop
to MRH's garage. They paid Andy the money for the car and we all signed the agreement
MRH now have 12 weeks to prove they car produce 200 bhp from the engine.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Tuesday&nbsp25th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Passed agreement past Solicitor for him to check and make any necessary ammendments.
MRH have no problem with it's content.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Monday&nbsp24th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Spoke to Solicitor and he thinks we did well to get the agreement.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Sunday&nbsp23rd</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Wrote draft version of agreement between myself, Andy and MRH. Emailed to Andy to check.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Saturday&nbsp22nd</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Travelled to London to meet Andy and MRH. After 3 hours of meeting they agreed to
(1) buy Andy's car off him and (2) use Andy's car to proof to me they could produce
200 bhp from it. If they could they would make the necessary changes to my car, if
they could not they would fit a standard engine and refund me the money,
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Friday&nbsp21st</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Andy managed to contact MRH and arranged a meeting with them at 9pm on Saturday night.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Thursday&nbsp20th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Received excellent letter from Solicitor to send to MRH.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Monday&nbsp17th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Went to see solicitor about MRH. He will write me a letter send to MRH asking them
what they propose to do to resolve the situation.
</TD>
</TR>


<TR><TD COLSPAN=2 BGCOLOR="#f00020"><CENTER>December 1999</CENTER></TD><TD>&nbsp</TD></TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Thursday&nbsp30th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
The starter motor has failed again. This is the second time since the engine
was rebuilt. My suspicion is that it is working too hard to turn the engine
over due to the very high compression ratio. Once again it fails just before
a holiday period leaving me without transport.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Monday&nbsp20th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
First real cold weather of the year and the water in the engine froze. My
suspicion is the MRH did not put any antifreeze in the engine (or not enough
anyway). Car overheated on way to work. Drained system in evening adding
a 50/50 mixture and all seems to be okay now.
</TD>
</TR>


<TR><TD COLSPAN=2 BGCOLOR="#f00020"><CENTER>November 1999</CENTER></TD><TD>&nbsp</TD></TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Tuesday&nbsp30th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Got letter off Mark at Owen Developments putting in writing the purpose and
findings of the rolling road session earlier in the month.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Thursday&nbsp18th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
The days finally arrived when I find out if the oil consumption is caused by
a mechanical or electrical problem. Mark spent two hours with the car. To
summerise, the engine had a tendancy to pink even with no alteration to the
timing. If the timing was increased the power rose but it has hampered by
serious detonation. This suggested that the compression ratio of the engine is
so high it's unsuitable to run to maximum efficiency on superunleaded
fuel. We ran a compression test on the four cylinders when warm and dry.
The gauge read up to 250 psi and a typical car would hit 170 - 180 psi.
When we tested my engine we got cylinder 1 - 245 psi, 2 - 235 psi,
3 - 245 psi, and cylinder 4 - 215 psi. Almost off the gauge. In conclusion
Mark said the timing and fueling of the car were fine and were not the cause
of the excessive oil consumption. Finally we did a power run. The car
produced a very dissapointing 165 bhp (140 bhp at the wheels) and
165 lbs/ft of torque.
I asked why Andy's car produced 196 bhp where as mine only produced 165 bhp.
Mark said that Andy's car did not actually produce that much more than mine
but MRH had asked him (in return for more business sent in his direction)
to artifically inflate the figures. Time to see a solicitor.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Tuesday&nbsp16th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
I phoned Mark at
Owen Developments as asked him to book me in for a rolling road session.
Mark said he could squeeze me in the morning of Thursday 18th November.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Monday&nbsp15th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Local garage finished fitting the new clutch. They also replaced the master
and slave clutch cylinders which I had asked them to do. The bill for just
the labour was &pound;337. I examined the old clutch and to my amazement the
pressure plate was the original FIAT item. I had paid MRH &pound;255 + VAT
to replace the clutch as part of the rebuild. For this amount I would have
expected a new pressure plate as well as a centerplate.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Friday&nbsp12th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Clutch finally turned up at midday but too last for local garage to finish
the job before the weekend.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Thursday&nbsp11th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Send Simon an Email this morning expressing my concerns as to whether the
clutch they had ordered would be able to withstand the power produced by my
car. Simon said he had checked and the clutch would be okay. The
replacement clutch still had not arrived by the end of the day so I
decided to phone up company that
makes the clutch that MRH ordered to see what they had to say. Discovered
that MRH had changed the order this morning. They had changed the centerplate
from one that can take 170 lbs/ft of torque to one that can take
200 lbs/ft. It was being shipped today and I would get it before midday
tomorrow.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Wednesday&nbsp10th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Local garage had removed old clutch and discovered the cause of the failure.
The lower arm of the clutch fork had broken. This caused the release
bearing to push against the pressure plate unevenly. This caused uneven and
excessive wear on the friction plate as it never fully disengaged. More
seriously it eventually caused the tube the release bearing slides along
to shear causing the whole mechanism to jam.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Tuesday&nbsp9th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Still no replacement clutch and car is booked onto rollers for Thursday
11th November. I spoke to Simon who had tried to contact the guy who made
the original clutch without success. Simon had phoned around and found
little alternative choice.
He is sending me a clutch made by Helix Autosport. It's a Grp N clutch
but only
rated at 170 lbs/ft of torque which isn't much more than what we are
currently seeing on my cars. Phoned Mark at Owen Developments to inform
him that the way things were going it was best to cancel the rolling
road session on Thursday 11th November.
</TD>
</TR>


<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Wednesday&nbsp3rd</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
On way to the rolling road session the clutch failed. It jammed engaged which
could have been very nasty as I was sat at a T junction waiting to pull
out. Took car to local garage to be repaired. Their initial diagnosis was that
it was simply the seal on the master clutch cylinder. However on further
examination it appeared that the release bearing had gone through the
pressure plate. This is a big job taking some 8 hours since the gearbox
needs to be removed. I had been compaining to MRH
ever since I got the car back from the rebuild that the clutch did not
seem right. I phoned Mark at the rolling road to cancel the session. He
rebooked me for Thursday 11th November. I spoke to Simon who said he would
send me a replacement clutch under warranty.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Tuesday&nbsp2nd</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
After speaking to Andy decided to go ahead with the rolling road session.
This is the only way we will know for sure whether its a mechanical or
electrical fault causing the high oil consumption.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Monday&nbsp1st</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Phoned Mark at Owen Developments to cancel the rolling road session on 
Wednesday 3rd. No point with the car burning as much oil as it is.
</TD>
</TR>


<TR><TD COLSPAN=2 BGCOLOR="#f00020"><CENTER>October 1999</CENTER></TD><TD>&nbsp</TD></TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Friday&nbsp29th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Finished off a tankful of fuel (40 litres) covering some 200 miles. Checked 
oil and nothing showing on dipstick. Added about a litre of new oil. Turned 
engine over to mix it up with the rest and checked dipstick. Oil is black 
not golden. Held rag over exhaust and it was black within seconds. Looks 
like car is still burning oil at the same old rate.
it at the same old rate.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Tuesday&nbsp26th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
In daylight I noticed the bonnet and offside front wing had several dents
and scratches in them which MRH had tried to cover up using the touch up
paint I keep in the glove compartment. That explained why they had washed the
car before my collection. Booked car onto rolling road at Owen Developments
for Wednesday 3rd November 1999.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Monday&nbsp25th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Had afternoon off work to collect car from MRH. They charged me &pound;431
for the labour and parts to fit the new valve guides and thermostat.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Friday&nbsp23rd</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Despite the Water pump arriving before noon on Wednesday Simon had been
unable to find time to fit it. The car was ready but by the time Simon had
informed me there was no way I could collect it before Monday 25th October.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Tuesday&nbsp19th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Spoke to Andy Forsdyke at Fiat Parts and he will send a water pump and 
gasket to Simon to arrive before noon. I told Simon he would get it 
tomorrow and he said he would phone me when it arrived at which point we 
could discuss whether he had time to fit it before Saturday.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Monday&nbsp18th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
I phoned Simon to ask whether the car is ready yet and whether he had a 
price for the water pump. Simon said that he had been unable to get a 
response out of his local Fiat dealer because they were being shut down. I 
said I would get the parts and send it to him. Simon said that he needed to 
get it by Wednesday 20th otherwise he would not have time to fit it.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Thursday&nbsp14th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
I phoned Simon to see if car would be ready for this weekend. Simon said it 
would not. I said then if they were going to have it all next week they 
should replace the water pump as well. I asked Simon to get me a price for 
the water pump and fitting.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Thursday&nbsp7th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
I phoned Simon to check on the rebuild.
I was originally told the rebuild would only take one week and it's
now the end of the fourth week. Yet another delay as Simon is waiting
for a new thermostat to arrive before the engine can be finished. This was
only ordered yesterday despite telling him that it needed a new one when
I took the car there. I was hoping to pick the car up on Saturday
9th but Simon thinks it will be more likely Monday 11th.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Friday&nbsp1st</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Simon phoned, they are two days behind with the rebuild. It will hopefully
be ready middle of next week.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR><TD COLSPAN=2 BGCOLOR="#f00020"><CENTER>September 1999</CENTER></TD><TD>&nbsp</TD></TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Tuesday&nbsp28st</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
I phoned Simon to see how the rebuild is going. Simon had now
reshimmed the head.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Tuesday&nbsp21st</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
I phoned Simon to see how the rebuild is going. The leak down test
on the cylinders gave an acceptable 8% leakage when the engine was cold.
The cylinders were not glazed. However a large amount of play was noticed
in the value guides. The guides have been replaced and the value seats
recut. The pistons have been removed, cleaned up and refitted.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Monday&nbsp13th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Took car down to garage so that they can sort out the large oil consumption
problem the car still suffers from. They said they will phone me the middle
of the week to let me know what the problem was and I should be able to
pick the car up on Saturday 18th.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Friday&nbsp10th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Could not take car to garage so arranged to take it there on Monday afternoon
instead.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Monday&nbsp6th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Simon phoned to check I was still bringing car to them on Friday 10th
September.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR><TD COLSPAN=2 BGCOLOR="#f00020"><CENTER>August 1999</CENTER></TD><TD>&nbsp</TD></TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Thursday&nbsp26th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
I phoned Simon and managed to get Friday 10th September as a date to bring
my car in to be looked at.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Wednesday&nbsp25th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Phoned Mike in afternoon, he was working on a car and said would phone
back in an hour.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Tuesday&nbsp24th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Spoke to Simon, he said that Mike was supposed to have phoned me.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Monday&nbsp23rd</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Spoke to Simon to try and get a date out of him for when I could bring
the car back to them to sort out the oil consumption. He said that he
had been away and that Mike was currently dealing with bookings but Mike
was out today. It's been four weeks now since they were supposed to be
making room for my car.
</TD>
</TR>


<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Saturday&nbsp14th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Andy Picked his car up from the electronic's expert. The work cost &pound;200 which included &pound;50 for a new battery. MRH were supposed to have
fitted a new one. The rest of the cost was diagnostic time to trace the
frayed wire from the crank shaft sensor. Andy can only assume that it got
damaged when he was dropping it off the trailer or while it was on the
rollers, although it should not have been so close to the pullys. The
car sounds awesome, like an F1 car. Andy only hopes it goes as well during
the race next weekend.</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Friday&nbsp13th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
The electric's guru has found the problem with Andy's car. The tiny thin
wire that runs to the crank sensor had been worn through by one of the
pulleys, maybe as a result of the rolling road session. For sure the car
sat very low on the rollers. Andy will pick it up tomorrow but has no idea
as to the cost.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Saturday&nbsp7th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Andy took his car to a car electronics expert to have them look at why
it won't run. There is no spark at the plugs.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Wednesday&nbsp4th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Andy worked on his car in preparation for a race at the weekend.
Changing the oil etc. The car would not idle at all, in fact it would not
run below 2000 rpm. He looked under the bonnet and found the cold start
valve lead hanging off. Andy plugged it back in and the motor then ticked
over at 3500 rpm, going crazy. It then went pop bang and died. It has not
run since.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR><TD COLSPAN=2 BGCOLOR="#f00020"><CENTER>July 1999</CENTER></TD><TD>&nbsp</TD></TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Friday&nbsp30th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Simon phoned to say that my rebuild will need to be delayed as they need
to sort out the mapping of a Ford Escort first. Simon will phone me sometime
during the next week to inform me when I can take the car to them.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Wednesday&nbsp28th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Andy took his car to Owen Developments to be remapped. It went very well
and his car is now producing 196 bhp compared to 188 with the old mapping.
The car sounds awesome now and feels very responsive. Andy will not
know for sure whether it is better to drive until his first race in a couple
of weeks. There is still a problem trying to get Andy's car to tick over
below 2000 rpm. Mark at Owen developments shut down the fuel as much as
possible but it's still throwing it in. Andy needs to blip the throttle
to keep it ticking over. Hopefully not too much of a problem for a race
car.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Tuesday&nbsp20th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
I phoned Simon to get some response on the letter I sent on 9th July.
Simon said that Unichip are very keen to sort out the problems with the
cars as they are worried that Dave Walker will write a uncomplementary 
article in Car and Car Conversions Magazine. Simon said they they had definately
booked Andy's car on Owen Developments rolling road on 28th July.
My car is booked in with MRH on 31st July to have the oil consumption
looked at again.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Wednesday&nbsp14th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Update from Andy. His fueling and ignition timings from the
chip were identical to mine. Very strange. He told MRH that they have to
take his car to Owen Developments as soon as possible to be remapped.
Mark at Owen Developments could not understand how Andy's car ran, let
alone set the times at Brands that it did. Which makes Andy wonder how
much more there is to come? Mike called Andy saying that his car is
booked onto Owen Development's rollers for 26th July.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Friday&nbsp9th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Wrote letter to Simon and Mike formally requesting that my oil consumption
needs to be addressed. I also noted that I'm extremely disappointed with
the performance of the car and want a refund to conpensate for achieved
against claimed power. Faxed a copy to be sure.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Friday&nbsp2nd</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Simon phoned to ask about the tuning session I had on
Wednesday. He is planning on talking to Mark at Owen Developments and
possibly taking Andys car there as he has run out of ideas as to why Andy's
car won't run properly at low revs. Once Andy's car is sorted they will
have a look at mine.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Thursday&nbsp1st</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Andy phoned. His car is still in the garage.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR><TD COLSPAN=2 BGCOLOR="#f00020"><CENTER>May 1999</CENTER></TD><TD>&nbsp</TD></TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Wednesday&nbsp30th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Took car to Owen Developments near Oxford to have the constant stalling
problem looked at and also why the car badly bogged down below 2500 rpm.
Owen Developments did a great job. The constant stalling was caused by
the throttle body not having an idle bleed value. The only way the
engine was able to tick over was by sucking air via the cold start
valve or around the edges of the throttle butterfly. Owen developments
have fitted an adjustable bleed value by customising a turbo boast
adjuster. One end of which is placed into the air filter and the
other into the pelum chamber. 
<BR>
When Owen Developments examined the mapping of the Unichip they found several problems
<UL>
<LI>There are two ignition/fuelling maps held by the Unichip,
one for full and one for part throttle. However because the
throttle position had not been calibrated correctly it would jump to
the full throttle curve too early.
<LI>The fuelling map was very strange as it had abrupt changes to the
fuelling. In the mid-range the fuel was reduced by 20% and at the high
end it was increased by 20%
<LI>For some, still unknown reason, Emerald Cams had not mapped the
Unichip below 2000 rpm. At 2400 rpm the ignition map jumped from 0 to 6
degrees of advance. It was this that caused the sluggish response until
2400 rpm was reached. Not wanting the waste time giving the car a
custom map they have put 4 degrees of advance across the whole rev
range. The car seems to be quite happy with this.
<LI>They have also set up the advance and retard for the tick over
since below 1600 rpm the Lamda sensor controls the fuelling.
If the revs drop below 900 rpm more and more advance is added.
Above 900 rpm more and more retard is supplied to keep the tick
over within range. This had not previously been set up.
<LI>The engine was only mapped up to 6400 rpm.
</UL>
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Wednesday&nbsp19th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Fitted a proper oil breather/separator to prevent any oil being sucked
into the air intake through the normal breather.
This is a very smart stainless steel canister.
Made no difference to oil consumption though. However the breather to
atmosphere on the canister was at quite a pressure showing that there
is huge air pressure being built up in the sump. This gives the impression
that the piston rings have failed.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Tuesday&nbsp11th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
I checked oil in car and it is still using some. I spoke to Simon and he said
the next thing to try would be the oil breather. On the Tipo this is connected
directly to the inlet manifold. It's possible that the oil is being sucked
out by the larger inlet manifold. What Simon
suggested was to disconnect this tube
and instead feed the it into a catchment tank. This should hopefully
eliminate any oil being drawn into the inlet manifold and we should also
be able to see if any oil is being forced out through the tube.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Monday&nbsp10th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Andy phoned to confirm it was the head gasket that had blown on his car. The
head is well on it's way to being fitted and is hoping to pick his car up
at the weekend.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Wednesday&nbsp5th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Now my car has been run in I took it to a local garage to have the
emissions testet. At tick over the HC content has come down by half and
at higher revs is now within the MOT allowed range. The HC content at
tick over is still very high but apparently this is not tested for an MOT,
only the HC figure at higher (2500 rpm) revs.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Saturday&nbsp1st</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Picked up car from garage. Very pleased that they replaced the head
gasket for free. I now have to run it in again for 500 miles. This
involves putting only small loads on the engine and keeping the revs
under 4000 rpm. Andy's car also blew it's head gasket today whilst
practicing for his race tomorrow. Andy took is car back to the garage
for them to strip it down.
</TD>
</TR>


<TR><TD COLSPAN=2 BGCOLOR="#f00020"><CENTER>April 1999</CENTER></TD><TD>&nbsp</TD></TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Thursday&nbsp29th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Simon phoned from garage. My car is all back together and ready to be
collected. I was amazed to hear that they had actually removed the pistons.
This was done in order to refinish the cylinders as they had been glazed
over by the burning oil.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Tuesday&nbsp27th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
I phoned Simon. The head has been removed and being prepared ready to
be refitted.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Tuesday&nbsp20th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Simon phoned from garage. He says that by using an endoscope on cylinder 4
he can see a stream of oil running down the side of the piston. This
seems to confirm that the head gasket is faulty.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Monday&nbsp19th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Simon phoned from garage. He has pressure tested the cylinders and they
are in very good condition producing 200 psi +/- 5 psi. So it looks like
the piston rings are okay and could be the head gasket instead.
This is good news as I won't need to run the
engine in again and it's not as big a job as feared.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Saturday&nbsp17th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Took car back to garage to have the oil consumption problem sorted out.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Wednesday&nbsp14th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Arranged with Simon to take the car to the garage at the weekend.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Tuesday&nbsp13th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Sent fax to garage asking for a quote on how long it would take to
fix the oil consumption problem. Got fax back from Simon saying that
in the worst case scenario (piston rings worn) it would take 2 weeks.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Friday&nbsp9th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Took car back to Emerald Cams to have the UNICHIP piggyback ECU reprogrammed.
They solved the intermittent hicup I had been expierencing below 2000 rpm
by completely disabling the chip below these revs.
</TD>
</TR>


<TR><TD COLSPAN=2 BGCOLOR="#f00020"><CENTER>March 1999</CENTER></TD><TD>&nbsp</TD></TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Monday&nbsp29th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Oil filter worked itself loose. It was a good job the car was parked when
I noticed it and that it did not undo completely whilst driving.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Saturday&nbsp20th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Took car for a pre MOT check up. Whilst at Aladdin Motorsport in Oxford the
main guy there had a look at the smoke and said it's highly likely the
piston rings have gone. He said that maybe they can't cope with the
compression the engine is running at. This might explain why the cars power
drops off from 5500 rpm.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Friday&nbsp19th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Simon phoned from garage. They can't fit my car in next week as they
have too much other work.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Wednesday&nbsp17th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Spoke to Simon. He said they would amongst other things run leakdown
and compression tests, check the oil pressure and visually inspect
the engine to determine where the oil is leaking from.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Sunday&nbsp14th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Another tankful of fuel used, still getting 28 mpg but also still
consuming 2 litres of oil. Car smokes quite badly under acceleration.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Thursday&nbsp11th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Another tankful of fuel used getting 28 mpg but also consuming 2 litres of
oil. I phoned Simon to inform him the car is still using a lot of oil.
He said he would talk to Mike about possible solutions so that they are
completely prepared for when I bring the car in.
He will phone me when they have dates available. Having driven the car
for some weeks I find that although the car can rev to 9000 rpm there
is little point. Above 7000 rpm the torque curve is falling away so
quickly that the car slows down and it's better just to change up a gear.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR><TD COLSPAN=2 BGCOLOR="#f00020"><CENTER>February 1999</CENTER></TD><TD>&nbsp</TD></TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Friday&nbsp26th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Since I have picked up the car I have covered 600 miles using two
tankfulls of fuel. Most worrying though is that it has also used
5 litres of oil. I can't see any oil leaking out of the engine so I guess
it must be being burnt or coming out as a vapour. On the good side I have
managed to get 29 mpg and 27 mpg out of the two tankfulls
which is pretty good for even the standard car.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Wednesday&nbsp24th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
I spoke to Simon about the cam profiles on my car. It's a Cosworth
DB3 but with unknown lift. Simon does not think any more lift is possible without
further cutouts in the pistons. He will try and track down Guy Croft as to
why the DB3 profile was chosen and whether a higher lift would help the car
breath better at the top end.
I spoke to Dave Walker and he says that a new Unichip should be arriving in
a couple of weeks. This is a modified unit
which will solve the problems encountered on my car below 2000
rpm.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Tuesday&nbsp23rd</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
The car smoking a lot. This is not good news. Took it to Aladdin
Motorsport in Oxford who had previously worked on the car. They ran the car
up on a gas analyser and noticed that below 2000 rpm the engine was not
burning the fuel properly. They also had a look at the power graphs and
were amazed when I told them it was the power at the flywheel. They had
expected it to be at the wheels. The engine should be producing far more
power than it is. One thing they suggested was to fit higher lift cams.
Aparently the Cosworth DB3 profiles are not particularly high lift and this
could well be why the car is loosing power higher up the rev range.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Saturday&nbsp20th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
The car is missfiring very badly high up the rev range. The garage said that
it only started to happen once the spark plug cover has been fitted back
on and is probebly the ignition leads being clamped. I brought some
replacement 8mm silicone leads and the problem is cured. The car seems
much faster now that it does not hiccup when acellerating.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Wednesday&nbsp17th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Asked Mike to make a catalytic converter replacement pipe as part of the custom exhaust
system. This would allow me to simply swap back in the catalytic converter
which the car will need for an MOT. Without this I would have to swap complete
exhaust systems and hence have to keep the old Supersprint system simply for
an MOT. I went to the garage in the afternoon to pick the car up. The total
bill is a frightening &pound;5798. Went for a drive in the car and it's
pretty quick and very loud.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Tuesday&nbsp16th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
The garage took the car back for the final time to the rolling road for it to
be remapped with the new exhaust system. The power and torque curves are
quite different from Andy's car and this can now only be down to the
different gas flowing of the cylinder head and the different throttle
body. The power is a reasonable 181 bhp at 6375 rpm but extremely
disappointing compared to the 230 bhp I was promised.
The torque curve low down is very strong but unfortunately 
peaks at 164 lbs/ft quite early at only
5187 rpm. Although much stronger than Andy's car up until this point Andy's
torque continues to pull for slightly longer hence the slightly higher
power he got of 188 bhp. Given that the car will rev to 9000 rpm They
calculated the maximum speeds in gear to be:
1st gear 48 mph, 2nd gear 75 mph, 3rd gear 110 mph, 4th 144 mph and
5th gear 187 mph. The garage have taken the car to the rev limit on 
the public road in 1st, 2nd, 3rd and 4th and can verify that the above
speeds are accurate.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Thursday&nbsp12th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Simon Phoned, they have finished fitting the new custom made
exhaust system. They have tried the car and it is now much freer reving.
It now feels like a totally different car and apparently goes very well.
0 - 60mph is rapid as they can now hit
45 mph in first gear and 80 mph in second gear. The cars
definately booked into the rolling on Tuesday 16th February.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Wednesday&nbsp11th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
I phoned Emerald Cams to see when their rolling road is available.
Next date will be Tuesday 16th February.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Monday&nbsp8th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
I spoke to Simon. They have decided to build the single silencer for the
exhaust system from scratch as they were not happy with the prefabricated
unit. The car is now provisionally booked into the rolling road on Thursday 12th February.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Friday&nbsp5th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Simon phoned. They had finished fitting the new exhaust camshaft and the
car performs much better now. They were currently making the new exhaust
system a bit more robust. They were hoping to book the car into Dave
Walkers Rolling Road on Tuesday 9th February. I spoke to Karl at Emerald Cams and he said he had not
received a new batch of Unichips yet, however they were expected any day.
He had spoken to the South African manufacturers and they had suggested a
few changes to the software program that they could try.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Wednesday&nbsp3rd</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
I spoke to Simon. They were finishing off fitting the new camshaft.
Aparently they had been waiting for a replacement seal.
Until the car is ready they won't book a rolling road session.
They don't know if the replacement UniChip has arrived either.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR><TD COLSPAN=2 BGCOLOR="#f00020"><CENTER>January 1999</CENTER></TD><TD>&nbsp</TD></TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Thursday&nbsp29th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
I phoned Simon. They have changed their plans regarding testing the
exhaust. They are now going to fit the different exhaust camshaft and then
take the car to Dave Walkers Rolling Road to be remapped. Whilst the car is
there they will try the different exhausts. Mike said that
although the Supersprint exhaust is 51mm (2&quot;) at points it is
restricted to only 42mm (1.7&quot;) whereas the exhaust
they are fitting is 64mm (2 1/2&quot;).
Andy says that his car has a 64mm (2 1/2&quot;)
race exhaust system fitted.
The new exhaust camshaft had arrived today and Simon would be fitting it
tomorrow.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Monday&nbsp25th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
I Spoke to Karl at Emerald Cams to see if he had any ideas as to why the
car was not performing as expected. Looking at the electrical side Karl
said that Andy's car was running at maximum between 10 and 12 degrees
of advance where as mine is was only 3 to 4 degrees. This shows that mine
is burning the fuel very quickly.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Friday&nbsp22nd</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Simon phoned from the garage. They are hoping to have the exhaust finished
on Monday 25th January. They can then take the car to a local rolling road
to see the difference in the power between the two exhausts.
They have sent a replacement exhaust camshaft away to be reprofiled and are
hoping to get it back early next week. They will fit this after the exhaust
tests and then take the car back to Dave Walkers rolling road for another
power run and to have the replacement Unichip fitted.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Tuesday&nbsp19th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
I spoke to my solicitor and I have two real options. I could pay the garage
the full amount stating that I intend to take the car to an independent
expert. If this expert finds any faulty parts or the cause of the lack
of power then I will expect the garage to reimburse me. Alternatively
I could pay
the garage a reduced final amount. This would take into account the lack of power
and what it might cost to put right. This is my preferred option as another
garage may well take it to pieces only to find nothing wrong with it and I
will end up with another hefty bill. Even if they find something wrong it
may prove very difficult to prove it and then recover the money
from the garage. I spoke to Simon and as it happens
the garage seem very keen to try and sort the problem out
at their expense. It seems sensible to let the garage have a go at solving
the problem as they know exactly how the engine has been designed. The
first thing they will try is to change the exhaust.
</TD>
</TR>


<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Sunday&nbsp17th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
I have decided, now that the car is at least
driveable, to get it back in it's current state. 
The garage have spent over 6 months building the
engine at a cost of &pound;4836 at it only produces 10 bhp more than when I
took it there. I plan on speaking to my solicitor on Monday 18th January to
discuss how good a case I would have for suing the garage for incompetence
and negligence. In the meantime I have phoned and faxed garage asking them not to spend any further time
or money on the car. In particular, not to order the different exhaust
camshaft nor to manufacture a different exhaust system.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Saturday&nbsp16th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
I phoned a guy called Peter at SP Developments (01564 - 782531) for 45
minutes. He was responsible for the original gas flowing of the head in
December 1997 which cost &pound;450 + VAT. Even though it was over a year
ago he
remembered the head very well as it stood out as a superb piece of
engineering design. It was flowing 130 cfm (Cubic feet per minute) and should
easily produce 230 bhp and with more effort 300 bhp should be possible.
Peter has flowed engines for over 25 years and has worked on anything from
a lawnmower up to a Ferrari. He has also worked with the 
Nissan Touring Car Team and is arguably the best in Europe. He
said that the Jaguar XJ6 throttle was a waste of time as even a 32mm restrictor
can flow 200 cfm. He couldn't understand how the garage could know what
profile of cams to fit without knowing the flow rate of the head nor the
valve lift of 11.5mm that the gas flowing was designed around. More importantly
he claimed that all of the work the garage had subsequently performed
on the head was detrimental to the work he had carried out. I phoned Mike
at the garage to confront him with this information. Not surprisingly he
was not happy that I was questioning the work he had carried out on the
head and said that the work he had carried out on the head had
been proven in engines and that flow benches could not simulate the real
world. Peter said that if I wanted him to flow another head for me he
would give me a good rate and also recommended a guy called David Wedge
(01865 - 407726) who would build my engine without questioning the
way the head had been ported. Never the less this is an expensive option
as a new head is &pound;775 + VAT.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Friday&nbsp15th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Mike and
Simon took the car to the rolling road again and a replacement
Unichip piggyback CPU was fitted. The
replacement unit didn't work properly either, it's as if it temporarily 
forgets what it's doing. The car is severely down on power, only producing
170 bhp and 142 lb/ft of torque. There are only three differences between
the engines, the Jaguar XJ6 throttle, the differently ported head and
the exhaust system. Mike is blaming the original porting work on the head, saying
that it is causing turbulence. Although this mixes the fuel/air mixture
really well, shown up by much less advance needed for my engine compared
to Andy's, it's slowing down the air going into the engine. He is proposing
to fit a different exhaust cam and removing two out of the three silencers
from the exhaust to reduce back pressure. I'm not too keen on this idea as
it will make the car extremely loud. I suggested fitting the old throttle
but he said they have already spent 5 man days at the rolling road and
were not prepared to take the car there again for power runs. Mike suggested
fitting NOS (Nitrous Oxide) costing an extra &pound;450 which 
he would fit it for free as a way of getting the car to
produce the power they had promised me.
I asked Mike to describe the changes he had made to the
gas flowed head that my car had originally. It turns out that they
didn't like what had already been done to the head and had
smoothed
the surface using 120 wet grid, made the short side radius as large as
possible, cut three angle valve seats, opened up valve seats to maximum
and recut the valve seats. So my head is now a combination
of the two porting techniques.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Wednesday&nbsp13th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Simon phoned from garage. The new fuel pump has arrived and been fitted.
The car now drives much better and quite happily revs to 8000 rpm.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Tuesday&nbsp12th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Simon and Mike took my car back to the rolling road at &quot;Emerald Cams&quot;
in London. However 
the fuel pump was not up to scratch and therefore not supplying the fuel under
enough pressure. It's only producing 1.75 bar of pressure where as it's
supposed to be 5.7 bar. There was also a problem with the Unichip
piggyback ECU that caused the engine to twitch which was very noticable
at tickover. Both faulty parts have been ordered and are being
delivered tomorrow. The car is booked back on the rolling road on Friday
15th January.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Monday&nbsp11th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
I Phoned Simon to check that the adjustable fuel pressure regulator had
been fitted okay. The unit Fiat use is the same as FORD use on thier
Zetec range of engines. He said it had been a nightmare to fit as it was tucked
away at the back of the engine. I also phoned &quot;Emerald Cams&quot;
and they had received the modified superchip.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Saturday&nbsp9th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
I collected a modified Superchip with the rev limit completely removed and
posted it to &quot;Emerald Cams&quot; ready for them to fit on
Tuesday 12th January.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Wednesday&nbsp6th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Andy finally agreed a total price of &pound;3750 with the garage for the work on his
car. This figure takes into account compensation for the time it's taken them. I contacted Dastek regarding whether
their Unichip product would allow a higher rev limit to be set than on the standard ECU
chip. It can be done by altering the clock frequency of the standard chip which
seems a bit of a hack and not very acurate. I therefore 
contacted Superchips and asked them
to blow me another chip with the rev limiter removed. The Unichip product
can then be used to set whatever rev limit we desire.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Tuesday&nbsp5th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Simon drove my car to &quot;Emerald Cams&quot; ready for my rolling road session.
However it would only run correctly on part throttle. Anything more than part
throttle resulted in a very lean fuel/air mixture. This is very encouraging
as it means that the larger throttle body is allowing in far more air. The
problem is that the injectors can't supply enough fuel for a complete
burn. Changing to larger injectors would solve the problem but is an
expensive option. We therefore opted to increase the fuel rail pressure.
Unfortunately the standard FIAT fuel pressure regulator, like most production
cars, is not adjustable. The standard fuel pressure regulator is in an awkward
position on the car so it will need to be taken away to be re-fitted.
Whilst my car was there I had fitted Dastek's Unichip product. This is a
piggy back ECU unit that allow the engine to be fully mapped. This cost
&pound;235 + VAT including fitting. The car was booked back on the rolling
road for Tuesday 12th January where they will increase the fuel pressure
and then program the Unichip. The paint job on the cam cover looked very
impressive. The noise from the larger throttle body was incredible. On
tickover the engine sounded like a large motorbike. Even
though the mixture was lean when opened up and put under strain the noise
from the air filter was not so much induction roar as machine gun fire.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Monday&nbsp4th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Andy took his car to &quot;Emerald Cams&quot; in Brixton London for tuning on their
rolling road. This is a very accurate/conservative rolling road and the
maximum power produced by his car was 188 bhp and 158 lb/ft of torque.
These are excellent figures and should hopefully be a
base line figure for my car given that Andy did not opt for the larger
throttle body.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR><TD COLSPAN=2 BGCOLOR="#f00020"><CENTER>December 1998</CENTER></TD><TD>&nbsp</TD></TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Wednesday&nbsp23rd</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Andy went to garage to pick up his car to take it to the rolling road
session at &quot;Emerald Cams&quot;.
However when he went to drive the car away the brakes had seized
and the power steering fluid was leaking. The car had not been run in enough
and needed a couple of hours on the rolling road just to loosen it up enough.
Dastek's Unichip piggyback ECU unit was fitted which took a couple of
hours. After a couple of hours of custom mapping the car produced a
disappointing 178 bhp. Andy booked his car in again for Monday 4th January
and mine for Tuesday 5th January. This should give Simon plenty of time
to get some miles on my car.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Monday&nbsp21st</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
It turns out that Dave Walker had not checked his fax machine and had
forgotten that we had booked our cars in for today or tommorow. The
rolling road is only available on Wednesday 23rd which I can't make so
Andy will go instead.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Thursday&nbsp17th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Andy phoned the garage but Simon was not sure whether the cars would be
run in fully in time for the rolling road session on Monday 21st December.
He was not sure whether himself or Mike would even be able to attend the
session.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Friday&nbsp11th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Simon phoned, my car is running fine and sounds awesome.
He is looking for another
powersteering belt as they have replaced all belts during the rebuild
but the standard one they had was too narrow for my car.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Wednesday&nbsp9th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Simon phoned and they have finished fitting the engine into the car.
He advised that although it's ready it should not go on the rolling road
in the morning as it needs to be thoughouly run in first.
The next rolling road
session available is 21st December so will book both cars in for then.
This should give the garage plenty of time to put some miles on the cars.
Andy is okay with waiting until then as it's important to test both cars
together.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Tuesday&nbsp8th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Car still not finished. Lots of little bits and pieces left to do such
as gearbox linkage, clutch cylinder, air and oil filters, radiator and
tighten alternator. The drive shaft nuts have stripped so will need new
ones. Simon thinks that the standard drive shafts will be up to the job.
Finally make contact with Dave Walker. He has a rolling road session
available to us on Thursday 10th and Monday 21st December.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Monday&nbsp7th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Simon phoned and they are almost about ready to start up my car. They
hope to be in a position to do this by tonight. They will leave it until
the morning though as they don't use antifreeze whilst checking for leaks.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Friday&nbsp4th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Simon phoned but said that work on my engine was very slow today
with too many distractions. He said he will lock the doors and
knuckle down to the job tomorrow. He is hoping the car will be finished by
Monday 7th December. Hopefully we can go down on Tuesday or Wednesday
depending on when the rolling road is available. Still no news however from
Dave Walker as to when his rolling road will be available.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Thursday&nbsp3rd</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Simon phoned to let me know he had a good day building my engine yesterday.
The cams have been fitted and timed. They had a slight delay whilst trying
to find somebody else to powder coat my cam cover as the usual guy they use
had died. I had not asked for the cam cover to be powder coated but it sounds
very smart indeed. The cover is electronically charged and then sprayed with
red paint. It is then baked in a very hot oven which gives it an almost
plastic like finish. The ribs along the top of the cover have been relieved
for extra effect.
Mike has finished the throttle body fabrication so it's pretty much ready
to be fitted back into car. Simon is now pretty hopeful that this will happen
on Saturday 5th December. They would like the car for an extra day
to drive it around to make sure there are no leaks etc.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Tuesday&nbsp1st</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
I spoke to Andy, he is not going to be away on business so would like his
car back as soon as possible. He will try and contact Dave Walker about
when his rolling road will be available to test the cars. I spoke to Simon
and he said that they will
try and get my car ready by the weekend. He will contact me Thursday
afternoon as to whether this looks likely. He does not envisage any
problems with the build it's just a matter of time.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR><TD COLSPAN=2 BGCOLOR="#f00020"><CENTER>November 1998</CENTER></TD></TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Monday&nbsp30th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
I phoned garage and spoke to Simon. He had planned to get my car ready by
12th December 1998 for the rolling road.
I said that I would like to collect
my car a week earlier on 5th December 1998. This would give me a chance to
thoroughly run my car in before the rolling road session.
Simon said he would phone me tonight as
to whether he thought this was possible.</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Saturday&nbsp28th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Simon phoned Andy to say that this car was ready and they were now
waiting for payment and collection. Andy said that as far as he was concerned
the work was not finished until he saw some rolling road power figures to
back up the work that they'd done. As agreed with myself earlier in
the week, Andy said that neither of us would pay for the cars until they
were both ready.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Thursday&nbsp26th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Andy phoned me with his good news that his car was ready.
We agreed that we had gone into this together and would only pay
the garage when both cars are finished. 
Andy is away on business from next Tuesday 1st December to Friday 11th
December. We would then take them on the rolling road on
Saturday 12th December. Hopefully my car will be ready a week
earlier on Saturday 5th December. I could then have use of the car for a
week before the rolling road session.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Wednesday&nbsp25th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
I spoke to Simon and he was very pleased to tell me that they had got
Andy's car up and running. They had taken the ECU to an electronics
engineer and he had noticed that the main chip had worked itself loose. Once
this had been pushed home the car started up with no problems. However, once
running they had noticed a water leak from a metal pipe that runs behind
the engine. They had to remove this, weld up the crack and refit it. This
had taken them the last three days to do. As a result, 
they had been unable to finish off my engine and therefore it
would not be ready for collection on Saturday 28th November. Simon said
that hopefully my car would be ready next Wednesday 2nd December.
I said that I would not be able to
collect it until Saturday 5th December so that should give them a
couple of days to bed down the engine and run it in for me.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Saturday&nbsp21st</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Andy and myself turned up on spec at the garage.
Andy's car was ready apart from some electronic sensors that had been
disconnected to carry out the wiring loom tests. Mike had tried to find
a spare ECU from scrap yards but it was proving difficult to find one whoose
serial numbers matched. My car however was still
without an engine and it looked far from being complete,
let alone being ready to fit back into the car.
To be fair to Mike and Simon they are perfectionists and had dry built
the engine 3 times already to get it perfect.
The con rods have a &quot;T&quot; cross section which is very
light but still provides ample strength.
The plenum chamber was still not ready as it had taken several
attempts to get it just right.
Mike agreed to try and get Andy's ECU tested. If nobody could be found
to perform these tests Andy said he would opt for the &pound;800 fully
mappable system being developed by Dave Walker. Mike said he would phone
Andy on Monday 23rd November to give him the news. Mike said that he would concertrate
his efforts into finishing off the rebuild of my engine and would be able to
tell me on Wednesday 25th November whether it would be ready by Saturday
28th November.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Wednesday&nbsp18th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Decided to give Venolia another call to get all the details of
when the forged pistons were paid for, how many were ordered etc.
Unfortunately, Venolia had not got details of when the garage had
originally ordered the pistons only when they received payment which was
1st October. That's 11 weeks after we gave the garage
the money. Venolia shipped the forged pistons on 12th October which means
they only took 1 working week to make. We know the garage received them
on 17th October. It therefore actually only took two weeks for Venolia
to manufacture the forged pistons and to have them delivered back to
the garage. The garage originally told us this normally takes between 4 and
6 weeks and was one of the disadvantages of chosing the forged pistons.
<BR><BR>
What's more, ours were not the only pistons the garage ordered. As well
as a set of 84mm and 84.6mm FIAT Tipo pistons for our cars the garage had
also ordered
a set of 86mm FIAT 2000 pistons and a set of 84.6mm FIAT 1800/2000 pistons.
The garage has also
ordered a set of 85.4mm FIAT 1800 pistons on 3rd November.
This also means that the garage ordered the forged pistons for Andy's car
even though at the time they paid for them (1st October) Andy had
already told them (17th September) that he wanted to go for standard cast pistons.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Tuesday&nbsp17th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
I spoke to Andy and agreed that the only way to find out what's really
happening with our cars is to go and visit the garage. This has been very
difficult for us to do until now due to our work loads. However, the situation
can't be allowed to go on any longer. An on spec visit would be the best
approach thus giving the garage no time to prepare for our visit.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Thursday&nbsp12th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Spoke to Simon at garage to get an update. Mike has finished the welding of
the plenum chamber and is currently setting up the throttle's potentiometer.
This could take another day as it appears to be a matter of trial and error.
The engine should then take a day or two to fit back into the car.
They appeared keen to get both cars out of the garage as soon as possible.
There is no news on Andy's car as it's been difficult
to obtain a spare ECU for testing however Simon is hoping that one will
arrive tomorrow.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Friday&nbsp6th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Spoke to Simon at garage to get an update. Andy's car is still not working. 
Simon has thoroughly checked the entire wiring loom and found no problems with it 
or any of the sensors. Simon is now trying to beg/steal/borrow another ECU as this is 
the only part left to try. Mike is still welding up the new plenum chamber for me.
Once the plenum chamber is ready it should only take a 
couple of days to put engine back into the car. Told it should be ready the end of next 
week or early the week after.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Tuesday&nbsp3rd</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Spoke to Simon at the garage. Mike is still welding together the new plenum 
chamber ready for the bigger throttle body. My engine is still not in the car as it's easier to fit it back 
on the inlet manifold before the engine is placed back into the car. Simon was 
currently working on why Andy's car won't start.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Monday&nbsp2nd</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Andy had been in contact with the garage. They have got his engine back 
together and fitted it into his car but they have been unable to start it. They think the 
electronic brain is damaged due to the violent crash the car underwent. Dave Walker 
at &quot;Car and Car Conversions&quot; magazine is keen to try a new ECU he has been 
working on when the car goes on the rolling road. This is a fully mappable device and 
should allow better tuning of the engine. Andy is away on business for two weeks and 
has asked the car to be ready for his return.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR><TD COLSPAN=2 BGCOLOR="#f00020"><CENTER>October 1998</CENTER></TD><TD>&nbsp</TD></TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Wednesday&nbsp28th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Andy phoned. He's back off holiday and heard nothing from the garage. He 
said that by changing the size of the throttle body the engine would need re-mapping. 
Given the other changes to the engine a re-map would be necessary anyway. Andy 
was surprised that his car was scheduled for the rolling road on Friday 30th October as 
he had asked them to tell him so he could be present.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Tuesday&nbsp27th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Spoke to Mike at the garage and gave him the go ahead for fitting the XJ6 
throttle body to my car. The end of the plenum chamber will need to be cut off to 
open it up more. A new mounting bracket will then need to be welded onto it in order 
to bolt on the larger diameter throttle body. They had tried this technique on the 
RS2000 and had now got the actual bhp figures back from the rolling road session. 
The power had increased from 176 to 198 bhp. Mike said that it would cost &pound;300, 
which seems a bit expensive for the work involved but on a pounds per bhp it's 
excellent value for money. They will be able to do this before it goes on the rolling 
road. This looks like it will now be towards the end of next week.
They are getting a new guy in to help out with the work.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Monday&nbsp26th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Spoke to Simon at the garage. The throttle body they will fit will come off a 
4.2 litre Jaguar XJ6. This is the largest production throttle body you can get and is 
some 12mm larger in diameter than the standard FIAT item. It should be easy enough 
to fit as looks exactly like the FIAT one including all the piping. Simon said that Mike 
would phone me with more details tonight.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Friday&nbsp23rd</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Spoke to Mike at the garage. The engine should be finished by Monday 26th 
October but they were unable to book it onto the rolling road until Monday 2nd 
November. I’m very disappointed as I was hoping to get it back by Saturday 31st 
October in time for my Wedding Anniversary. Mike talked about increasing the size 
of the throttle body to allow more air in. This should give an extra 15-20 bhp and the 
induction roar should sound amazing. Mike thinks it will cost roughly &pound;250 for labour 
and parts and will work on an exact price for me.
They 
had a Ford RS2000 2.0 litre 16v with a similar specification engine (without the
re-profiled camshafts) but it only produced about 180 bhp. The garage have replaced my
crankshaft as the old one was damaged, there’s not a lot of the original engine left. 
The compression ratio has been calculated as 11.6:1, any higher and will get
pre-detonation at lower revs.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Friday&nbsp16th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Spoke to Mike at garage, he had checked with UPS and the forged pistons 
are clearing customs today. They are going to collect them in the morning.
At some point UPS had lost
the consignment and Mike had spent the whole week tracking them down. 
Simon is hoping to rebuild the engines on 
Saturday 17th October and Monday 18th October. Hopefully it will be ready by next 
weekend. Andy however, finally cracked, enough was 
enough. He phoned Mike saying that he was extremely angry, three months to rebuild 
the engines is beyond a joke. The garage has ruined their reputation as neither of us 
feel at all like recommending them.
Andy gave them one week to rebuild the cars.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Friday&nbsp9th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Phoned the garage, Simon would not confirm the current state of engine only 
that it was not in the car. The inlet manifold was not connected as they were testing 
the injectors, all of which appeared to be working perfectly. Simon was hopeful that 
the forged pistons would arrive on Monday 12th October or Tuesday 13th October. 
They will only book the car onto the rolling road when engine back together. Simon 
would not disclose when this might be.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Thursday&nbsp1st</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Andy spoke to Simon at garage.
He would not tell Andy the current state of his car but said it was probably 
going to be ready on Wednesday 7th October and going on the rolling road on Friday 
9th October.I phoned Venolia in USA 
again, they had only received payment for the pistons today
11 weeks after we gave the garage the deposit.
The paperwork 
was currently being typed up so they could not give me any details such as how many 
pistons were being made. They should be ready in between two and two and a half 
weeks. Delivery can be next day or up to a week, it depends on the client.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR><TD COLSPAN=2 BGCOLOR="#f00020"><CENTER>September 1998</CENTER></TD><TD>&nbsp</TD></TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Wednesday&nbsp30th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Phoned the garage, they said they would hopefully have the car ready by 
Friday 2nd October. They are still hopeful that the Venolia forged pistons will arrive 
on Wednesday 7th October and will be able to place them straight in.
The advantage of the forged pistons is that they 
can withstand 9000 rpm whereas standard cast pistons will only take about 7500 rpm.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Friday&nbsp18th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Andy phoned as promised before speaking to Simon. We agreed not to say 
anything to them about the Venolia forged pistons until we get the cars back.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Thursday&nbsp17th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Simon phoned. They realize it’s taking a long time and have come up with a plan B 
for Andy who is keen now to get the car back. This is basically the same specification 
engine except using standard cast pistons. These will take two days to arrive and the 
engine should be finished in a couple of weeks. I opted to stick with the forged 
pistons but needed to know just how much longer they will take. If it were going to 
take much longer I would opt for the cast pistons as well. I phoned Simon at the 
garage, he is reluctant to phone Venolia as he thinks they will put our order to the 
bottom of the pile if we hassle them.
I thought it can’t get any worse 
and it’s not as if we’ve been pestering Venolia for when the pistons would be ready. I 
looked up Venolia in USA on the Internet and gave them a call to get some details. I 
was horrified to hear that Venolia have not even started making the pistons. They are 
waiting for full payment from the garage before starting the work as it is an overseas 
delivery.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Wednesday&nbsp16th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Andy phoned the garage but they didn't have any news.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Monday&nbsp14th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Andy phoned the garage, they have still not received the forged pistons.
We are keen to get our cars back and may be tempted to opt for the standard cast pistons.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Friday&nbsp4th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
I phoned the garage but they have still have not received the forged pistons. 
Apparently they are now in shipping.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR><TD COLSPAN=2 BGCOLOR="#f00020"><CENTER>August 1998</CENTER></TD><TD>&nbsp</TD></TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Wednesday&nbsp26th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
I phoned the garage, they have still not received the forged pistons and con 
rods. Apparently the forged pistons have been made, they are just waiting for the 
piston pins and rings. The garage has performed some extra mild porting work on the 
cylinder head but I won't be charged for this, as it was not in the original quote.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Friday&nbsp14th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
I phoned the garage to check everything is going okay. They have removed the 
engine from car, stripped it down and it’s currently being cleaned in chemicals. They 
are still waiting for the con rods and forged pistons from Venolia.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Sunday&nbsp2nd</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Hired a trailer for &pound;40 and got a colleague from work to tow it to the 115 miles 
to the garage. I had difficulty getting the car onto the trailer, as the car was so low. 
The garage would not give me a time scale for when the work would be finished but 
said they would know more once the forged pistons and con rods had arrived.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR><TD COLSPAN=2 BGCOLOR="#f00020"><CENTER>July 1998</CENTER></TD><TD>&nbsp</TD></TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Wednesday&nbsp22nd</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
I phoned the garage to see if they had received the cheque. They had received 
it yesterday evening as they had missed the postman. I arranged to deliver the car to 
the garage on Sunday 2nd August.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Monday&nbsp20th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
I wrote up a modified version of the original quote from the garage and posted 
it back to them together with a deposit of &pound;2000.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Saturday&nbsp18th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
I phoned Andy to find out how the meeting went with the garage on Thursday 
16th July. He said it went very well and had managed to get them to reduce the price 
of the engine rebuild from &pound;950 to &pound;750. I spoke to the garage, they want &pound;2000 
deposit from each of us to be paid as soon as possible so that they can order parts. The 
parts will take four weeks to arrive, as the forged pistons need to be made by Venolia 
in USA. They are going to prepare Andy's car for the next two weeks and then they 
will prepare mine for the next two weeks. In a months time they will have all the parts 
and can then start to build both engines at the same time. Good news is that they have 
a spare new pair of cams off a 16V FIAT Coupe that they can use as donors to
re-profile for my engine.
</TD>
</TR>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>Thursday&nbsp16th</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP>
Andy had meeting with the garage to discuss the
<A HREF="specification.html">specification</A>
we want them 
to build the engines to and to negotiate prices for the work
</TD>
</TR>


